### Describe the bug

`em.remove(entity)` + `flush()` deletes the row, but **any later `flush()` on the same `EntityManager` re-inserts it** with its original primary key.

The trigger is a referrer that holds the removed entity in its **primary key** and is still resident in the identity map — for example one whose row was deleted outside the ORM (`nativeDelete`, raw SQL, or another EntityManager), so it is not evicted along with its target.

No error is raised. Unless the resurrected row collides with a constraint or a trigger, the write goes through silently.

### Reproduction

Self-contained, in-memory SQLite:

```ts
import { Entity, ManyToOne, PrimaryKey, Property, ReflectMetadataProvider } from '@mikro-orm/decorators/legacy';
import { MikroORM } from '@mikro-orm/sqlite';

@Entity()
class Foo {
  @PrimaryKey()
  id!: number;

  @Property()
  label!: string;
}

/** Referrer whose primary key is the relation itself. */
@Entity()
class Referrer {
  @ManyToOne(() => Foo, { primary: true })
  foo!: Foo;
}

test('a removed entity is not re-inserted by a resident referrer', async () => {
  const orm = await MikroORM.init({
    dbName: ':memory:',
    entities: [Foo, Referrer],
    metadataProvider: ReflectMetadataProvider,
  });
  await orm.schema.refresh();

  const em = orm.em.fork();
  const foo = em.create(Foo, { label: 'foo' });
  em.create(Referrer, { foo });
  await em.flush();

  // the referrer row is deleted outside the ORM, so it stays resident in the identity map
  await em.nativeDelete(Referrer, { foo: foo.id });

  await em.remove(foo).flush();
  await expect(orm.em.fork().count(Foo)).resolves.toBe(0); // passes

  // any later flush on the same EntityManager resurrects it
  await em.flush();
  await expect(orm.em.fork().count(Foo)).resolves.toBe(0); // fails: 1

  await orm.close(true);
});
```

`nativeDelete` is not the trigger; it is just the shortest way to reach the state. The same failure occurs with no `nativeDelete` anywhere — one EntityManager loads the referrer while a second one deletes it with a plain `em.remove(referrer).flush()`. What matters is only that a referrer holding the removed entity in its primary key is still resident while its row is gone.

`primary: true` is what makes the difference: give `Referrer` its own surrogate `@PrimaryKey()` and keep the identical `@ManyToOne(() => Foo)`, and the second flush correctly emits nothing.

### Expected behavior

The second `flush()` is a no-op. An entity explicitly removed and evicted in an earlier flush stays removed.

### Actual behavior

The second `flush()` computes a `CREATE` change set for the already-removed entity and issues an INSERT carrying its original primary key:

```
--- flush 1: remove(foo) ---
[cs] delete Foo 1 {}
delete from `foo` where `id` in (1)
identity map: ["Referrer-1"]

--- flush 2: bare flush ---
[cs] create Foo 1 {"id":1,"label":"foo"}
insert into `foo` (`id`, `label`) values (1, 'foo')
identity map: ["Referrer-1","Foo-1"]
```

### Where it goes wrong

`UnitOfWork.unsetIdentity()` does try to scrub referring relations, but assumes the value is accessor-backed:

https://github.com/mikro-orm/mikro-orm/blob/2f4d739a5/packages/core/src/unit-of-work/UnitOfWork.ts#L711-L715

```ts
if (prop.formula) {
  delete referrer[prop.name];
} else {
  delete helper(referrer).__data[prop.name];
}
```

`EntityHelper.decorate()` returns early for `prop.primary` and defines no accessor, so a primary relation is a plain own property that never reaches `__data`:

https://github.com/mikro-orm/mikro-orm/blob/2f4d739a5/packages/core/src/entity/EntityHelper.ts#L153

Inspecting the referrer between the two flushes confirms it:

```
referrer.foo still set? true   in __data? false   own prop? true
```

The `delete` is therefore a no-op, the referrer keeps a live reference to the removed instance, `findNewEntities()` reaches it through `processReference()` on the next flush, and because `unsetIdentity()` also cleared `__originalEntityData` the comparator classifies it as a new entity and emits `CREATE`.

Changing that branch to `prop.formula || prop.primary` makes the phantom INSERT disappear, which confirms the path — but it is not a fix, since a primary key component cannot meaningfully be unset (it leaves an empty `UPDATE` change set for the referrer). The removal state itself probably needs to be sticky, so a flush refuses to re-create an entity the same unit of work explicitly removed, regardless of what still points at it.

### History

This is not a recent regression. The repro above resurrects the row on every version tried:

| version | change sets on the second flush | rows after |
| ------- | ------------------------------- | ---------- |
| 7.0.17  | `delete Foo pk=1`, `create Foo pk=1` | 1 |
| 7.1.4   | `delete Foo pk=1`, `create Foo pk=1` | 1 |
| 7.1.5   | `delete Foo pk=1`, `create Foo pk=1` | 1 |
| 7.1.13  | `delete Foo pk=1`, `create Foo pk=1` | 1 |

It can nevertheless present as a 7.1.5 regression, which is how we hit it. Before #7904 (released in 7.1.5), `getChangeSetGroups()` keyed its groups by the `EntityMetadata` instance while `getCommitOrder()` deduped by `_id`. When one flush carried change sets whose metadata instances differed for the same `_id`, a whole group could miss the lookup in `persistToDatabase()` and be discarded through its `?? []` fallback — no SQL, no error. In our application the phantom `CREATE` landed in such a group, so it was computed on 7.0.17 but never executed. Once #7904 made both sides key on `_id`, the same change set started reaching the database.

So the upgrade did not introduce the resurrection; it stopped suppressing the write. The minimal repro above emits a single change set on the second flush, which cannot miss that lookup, so it fails identically on both sides of 7.1.5.

