## Related issues

- #7317 — Polymorphic relation with default filter on one target creates an incorrect INNER JOIN, fixed in v7.0.3 by #7332
- #7563 — Polymorphic relation with TPT sub-class targets (INSERT/SELECT bugs), fixed in v7.0.11 by #7564

Both fixes are present in v7.0.15. The remaining case described here involves **all three features simultaneously**: polymorphic `manyToOne`, TPT sub-class targets, and any default filter whose condition references a column that exists only on the TPT parent table (not on child sub-tables). The reproduction below uses a soft-delete filter as a concrete example, but the same crash occurs with any filter in this position.

## Problem

When a `Part` entity holds a polymorphic `manyToOne([DeviceA, DeviceB])` where both targets are TPT sub-classes of an abstract `Device` parent, and the parent carries a default filter whose condition references a parent-table column (here: an embedded audit property resolved to `audit_deleted_at`), populating the inverse collection (`DeviceA.parts`) throws:

```
InvalidFieldNameException: column d0.audit_deleted_at does not exist
```

In TPT, the embedded `audit_deleted_at` column lives on the parent table (`device`). The child sub-tables (`device_a`, `device_b`) do not have it. MikroORM's polymorphic join code path applies the filter condition directly to the child sub-table alias, bypassing the parent table join that would ordinarily carry the column.

## Entity setup

```typescript
const AuditEmbeddableSchema = defineEntity({
  name: "AuditEmbeddable",
  embeddable: true,
  properties: {
    deletedAt: p.datetime().nullable(),
  },
});
class AuditEmbeddable extends AuditEmbeddableSchema.class {}
AuditEmbeddableSchema.setClass(AuditEmbeddable);

// Any default filter whose cond references a parent-table-only column triggers
// the bug. Soft-delete is used here as a concrete example.
const PARENT_COLUMN_FILTER = {
  excludeSoftDeleted: {
    name: "excludeSoftDeleted",
    cond: { audit: { deletedAt: null } },  // resolves to `audit_deleted_at`
    default: true,
  },
};

// Abstract parent — `audit_deleted_at` lives in THIS table only
const DeviceSchema = defineEntity({
  name: "Device",
  tableName: "device",
  abstract: true,
  inheritance: "tpt",
  filters: { ...PARENT_COLUMN_FILTER },
  properties: {
    id: p.integer().primary(),
    name: p.string(),
    audit: () => p.embedded(AuditEmbeddable).onCreate(() => new AuditEmbeddable()),
  },
});
class Device extends DeviceSchema.class {}
DeviceSchema.setClass(Device);

// Child TPT entities — sub-tables hold `id` only, NOT `audit_deleted_at`
const DeviceASchema = defineEntity({
  name: "DeviceA",
  tableName: "device_a",
  extends: Device,
  properties: {
    id: p.integer().primary(),
    parts: () => p.oneToMany(Part).mappedBy("parentDevice"),
  },
});
class DeviceA extends DeviceASchema.class {}
DeviceASchema.setClass(DeviceA);

const DeviceBSchema = defineEntity({
  name: "DeviceB",
  tableName: "device_b",
  extends: Device,
  properties: { id: p.integer().primary() },
});
class DeviceB extends DeviceBSchema.class {}
DeviceBSchema.setClass(DeviceB);

// Polymorphic owning side
const PartSchema = defineEntity({
  name: "Part",
  tableName: "part",
  properties: {
    id: p.integer().primary(),
    parentDevice: () => p.manyToOne([DeviceA, DeviceB]).ref(),
  },
});
class Part extends PartSchema.class {}
PartSchema.setClass(Part);
```

## Steps to reproduce

```typescript
await orm.em.find(DeviceA, {}, { populate: ["parts"] });
```

`EntityLoader.findChildren` issues the child-find query for `Part`. Because `Part.parentDevice` is polymorphic, MikroORM JOINs both target sub-tables and applies their inherited default filter to each join. The generated SQL reads (PostgreSQL):

```sql
SELECT "p0".*
FROM "part" AS "p0"
LEFT JOIN "device_a" AS "d0"
  ON "p0"."parent_device_id" = "d0"."id"
  AND "p0"."parent_device_type" = 'device_a'
  AND "d0"."audit_deleted_at" IS NULL      -- ← does not exist on device_a
LEFT JOIN "device_b" AS "d1"
  ON "p0"."parent_device_id" = "d1"."id"
  AND "p0"."parent_device_type" = 'device_b'
  AND "d1"."audit_deleted_at" IS NULL      -- ← does not exist on device_b
WHERE "p0"."parent_device_id" IN (1)
  AND "p0"."parent_device_type" = 'device_a'
```

## Expected behaviour

Any column referenced in a default filter on the TPT parent entity belongs to the parent table (`device`). The filter condition should be resolved against the parent table, not against the child sub-table alias. For a regular TPT query MikroORM correctly joins the parent table and uses its alias; the polymorphic join code path does not, causing any filter column that lives only on the parent table to be unresolvable.

## Actual behaviour

The query references `device_a.audit_deleted_at` (and `device_b.audit_deleted_at`). Those columns do not exist. PostgreSQL throws `InvalidFieldNameException: column d0.audit_deleted_at does not exist`; SQLite throws `no such column: d0.audit_deleted_at`.

